%n [...] ... [ša hu]mbāba | rigma | kī? [...]
// L I.4 SB "V" 103
// L I.4 OB "Schøyen₂" 57

%n [... | i]mtanaqqutū | eli! burkīšu
// cf. L II.2 SB "II" 78
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 30
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 44

%n [... | ša]knat | ekletu | ina ma[hri ...]
#note: The reading follows @bib{RN271@248}.
// cf. (Gilgameš and Huwawa A 77)

%n [ibru? | ištēn | ištē]n-<ma> | u šina | šinā-ma
#note: See @bib{RN271@249}: “The context is already clear in the first preserved line of MB b, which I prefer to read (l. 1'): @akk{[... iš-te]-en# u₃ ši-na ši-na-ma?#}. This can be compared with SB V “72” [= 75]: @akk{[x] iš-ten₂ iš-ten₂-ma š[i?-...]}. Perhaps the two fragments of lines can be put together: [@i{ša}] @i{ištēn ištēnma u šina šināma} “He who is one is just one; but two are two”.
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 75

%n [...] | lubārātu-mi! | šitta | šahṭūt[u!]
#note: See @bib{RN271@249}: “Continuing backwards, I suggest that the beginning of l. 3' (Arnaud: @akk{[...] x da-ra-ta mi-ši-ta ša ih-ṭu₂-pu}) is to be read as @akk{lu#-ba!-ra-ta-mi ši-ta ša-ah-ṭu₂-t[a]} “a pair of garments taken off” (note false agreement); cf. SB V “73” [= 77]: [2] @i{lubārātumma} […]. The episode is also known from the piece MB Emar₁ (@bib{RN117@328–31}). This is also a pitiful fragment but we read rev. iii 7′: @akk{[...] 2{+ta} ša-ah-na}, which may be a corrupt version of the same line.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 76
// cf. L I.4 MB "Emar₁" 7'

%n ašlu | šākultum | harru? | ... [...]
#note: As noted by @bib{RN271@249}, the line is a corruption of the SB text (V 79): @i{ašlu šušlušu} […], “a three-ply rope [@i{is not easily broken}.]”.
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 79

%n [napih]ta | bēr | šalû | ṣarihta | bēr | litapput[u!]
#note: An infinitive verb with a meaning “to kindle” or similar is expected at the end of the line. With respect to @i{ṣariḫtu}, as a variant for @i{tumru} in the SB version, see @bib{RN271@250}: “@i{ṣariḫtu} is evidently a feminine adjective in use as an abstract noun, meaning ‘fiery heat’.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 134

%n ina rāṭi (|) danni [...]
#note: As noted by @bib{RN271@250}, @akk{i-na ra-ṭi dan-ni} is a corrupted version of the @i{ina rāṭu} (|) @i{nappāḫi} in the SB text.
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 133

%n [... | lā l]ēʾû | awīlu | u attā
#note: The reading follows @bib{RN271@250}.
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 116

%n [... | ša? lā?] idû | abāšu?
#note: The emendation follows the parallel in the SB version. It is possible that the scribe erroneously repeated the signs occurring at the end of the preceding line, i.e. @akk{AT-TA}. 
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 117

%n [iššīšunūtī-ma? | šamû? | rū]qū?
#note: The first sign, read as @akk{BI} by @bib{RN88@136}, could also be read as @akk{QU}: the line would then fit SB V 153–154): @i{iššīšunūtī-ma} [@i{šamû}?] @i{rūqū} / @i{imhaṣ qaqqaram-ma pīlu uštamhiršu}.
// L I.4 SB "V" 153

u ... | uhtepp[â? | sirara? | u labnānu?]
#note: The proposed reading is based on the SB version (@akk{ina sa-a-ri-šu₂-nu uh-tap{+te}-pu-u₂ si-ra-ra u la-ba-na-nu}), although the traces visible in Arnaud’s copy are difficult to reconcile with the SB text.
// L I.4 SB "V" 156

