%n [enk]īdu | itti būli | ṣalil-ma | ul alsâ | e[līšu]
#note: See @bib{RN271@253}: “The first of these couplets is a lyrical evocation of a bygone time when the world was unsullied. It does not fit the present passage well, and may be an intrusion. Its recall of Enkidu’s youth is reminiscent of a line much earlier in the heroes’ encounter with Ḫumbaba (…).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 119

